// Cross-variant capability parity for the Orca X product family.
//
// Why this exists separately from the two manifests beside it: reliability-gates.jsonc disciplines
// *claims* (a gate may not assert protection without a real test and a passed run), and
// durability-contracts.jsonc answers *what a write owes* if its state is lost. Neither asks the
// question this one does -- when a capability improves on one variant, does the sibling variant
// inherit it, and if not, why not?
//
// That question needs its own registry because nobody was asking it. Measured 2026-09-08: 195
// fork-local commits since the upstream fork point e4e54a17d, 367 files changed, and *zero* of them
// touch mobile/. The mobile app is not behind because it is primitive -- it is 743 source files and
// 111k lines with a stronger transport layer than the desktop's. It is behind because every
// improvement landed on the desktop side of a wire that never learned to describe it.
//
// Parity here is SEMANTIC, never file-based. "Desktop changed file X so mobile must change file Y"
// is not a rule this gate can state or would want to. A capability may be shared code, a shared
// protocol, a different presentation of one invariant, or genuinely inapplicable. What the gate
// refuses is the fifth case: nobody checked.
//
// dispositions:
//   shared-already   One implementation serves both variants, or one mirrors the other under a test
//                    that fails when they drift. Requires `evidence`.
//   ported           The desktop behaviour now exists in mobile. Requires `evidence`.
//   equivalent       Mobile expresses the same user capability and invariant through a different
//                    presentation. Requires `evidence` AND `invariant`.
//   desktop-only     The capability cannot exist on mobile by nature. Requires `reason`.
//   not-applicable   Mobile has no such surface. Requires `reason`.
//   superseded       Mobile already solves this, at least as well. Requires `reason`.
//   blocked          Real gap with a named external blocker. Requires `reason` and `owner`.
//   measured-open    Investigated, gap named, nothing external blocking it -- it just is not built.
//                    Requires `evidence` (what was measured) AND `reason` (what remains and what
//                    closes it). Ratcheted separately from unknown.
//   unknown          Nobody has looked yet. Ratcheted -- see unknownPolicy.
//
// Why unknown and measured-open are two states and not one: they need different actions. An unknown
// needs someone to investigate; a measured-open gap needs someone to implement. Collapsing them
// understates what has been learned and hides which of the two a capability is waiting on. The
// hazard that comes with splitting them is relabelling -- calling a row "measured" and booking it as
// progress -- so the gate counts BOTH lists in one unresolved total, and that total only falls when
// a capability is genuinely resolved. Knowing more is not the same as having built it.
//
// severity is optional and drawn from `severities`. An unresolved capability without one counts as
// P0 in the release gate: forgetting to triage can only make the gate stricter.
//
// severityReason is REQUIRED on an unresolved capability whose severity is not P0, because that is
// the only assertion in this file that moves the release number without building anything.
// `unresolved` cannot be gamed -- it sums both debt lists, so relabelling leaves it where it was.
// `release-critical` could be, and was: on 2026-09-10 four triages took it from 5 to 1 with no
// product code and nothing required alongside. The field cannot check that an argument is a good
// one; it insists that someone wrote one down where a reviewer will find it, which is the
// difference between a judgement and a keystroke.
{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "updatedAt": "2026-09-08",
  "policy": {
    "variants": ["desktop", "mobile"],
    "dispositions": [
      "shared-already",
      "ported",
      "equivalent",
      "desktop-only",
      "not-applicable",
      "superseded",
      "blocked",
      "measured-open",
      "unknown"
    ],
    "evidenceRequiredFor": ["shared-already", "ported", "equivalent", "measured-open"],
    "reasonRequiredFor": ["desktop-only", "not-applicable", "superseded", "blocked", "measured-open"],
    "invariantRequiredFor": ["equivalent"],
    // The two kinds of debt. Summed for the unresolved total the gate prints on every run.
    "unresolvedDispositions": ["unknown", "measured-open"],
    "severities": ["P0", "P1", "P2", "P3"],
    // Ratcheted, not reported: config/variant-parity-unknown-baseline.txt and
    // config/variant-parity-measured-open-baseline.txt grandfather the capabilities that predate
    // each gate and may only shrink. Existing debt stays visible while a NEW entry in either state
    // fails. "There were already some" is not an argument a future change can make.
    "unknownPolicy": "ratchet",
    "measuredOpenPolicy": "ratchet"
  },
  "capabilities": [
    {
      "id": "agents.catalog-membership",
      "title": "Both variants offer the same set of agents, in the same order",
      "disposition": "shared-already",
      "evidence": ["mobile/src/tasks/mobile-agent-catalog.test.ts"],
      "notes": "Mobile mirrors src/shared/tui-agent-selection.ts and the test reads the desktop source across the project boundary to assert it. Measured in parity at 36 agents on 2026-09-08."
    },
    {
      "id": "agents.display-label",
      "title": "An agent carries the same user-facing name on both variants",
      "disposition": "shared-already",
      "evidence": ["mobile/src/tasks/mobile-agent-catalog.test.ts"],
      "notes": "The fork renamed the Codex launch row to ChatGPT in the renderer catalog alone, so the same agent had two names in one product. The membership test could not see it: it compared order and set, never labels. Widened 2026-09-08, which is the drift this registry was opened on. Desktop still labels Codex as 'Codex' on non-launch surfaces (notifications, CLI, usage provider); that is a deliberate scoping decision by fe30a7426, not drift."
    },
    {
      "id": "terminal.launch-profile-offer",
      "title": "Both variants offer the same set of launch profiles, from one composer",
      "disposition": "ported",
      "evidence": ["mobile/src/session/mobile-launch-profile-parity.test.ts"],
      "notes": "Desktop composed agents, shells and user-declared profiles; mobile answered the same question from its own agents-only list, so a declared profile existed on one variant only. The composer now lives in src/shared/terminal-launch-profiles.ts and both variants read it, so a new agent reaches both from one edit. No wire change was needed: session.tabs.createTerminal already carried command, env, delivery and an idempotency key over a required worktree, and terminalLaunchProfiles already rode in the settings payload mobile fetches. Mobile deliberately drops shell rows (it has its own plain-terminal row and no Windows shell menu) and keeps the diff-notes list agents-only (a declared ssh session cannot receive a prompt)."
    },
    {
      "id": "terminal.launch-profile-identity",
      "title": "A terminal can say which launch profile it came from, and tell recorded from inferred",
      "disposition": "measured-open",
      "severity": "P2",
      "severityReason": "No mobile surface consumes the distinction: there is no relaunch-from-this-tab affordance anywhere in mobile/src or mobile/app, so nothing presents an inference as a fact today. This is a gap to close BEFORE mobile grows such an affordance, and it becomes release-critical on the day one lands.",
      "evidence": [
        "src/shared/terminal-launch-profile-identity.ts",
        "src/shared/runtime-types.ts",
        "mobile/src/session/mobile-terminal-tab-agent.ts"
      ],
      "reason": "Traced 2026-09-10, and this is the one capability on this list where the fact genuinely does not reach the client -- the opposite of the four before it. The identity is a pure derivation over three tab fields: launchProfileId, launchAgent and shellOverride. launchAgent rides on the runtime session-tab row and mobile already consumes it, preferring it over the title (resolveMobileTerminalTabAgentId reads hook status, then launchAgent, and only then falls back to a glyph -- so the old note here, that mobile infers identity from a title glyph, understated what mobile does). launchProfileId is the ONLY field that yields source:'recorded', it exists on TerminalTab and in the persisted session schema, and it appears nowhere under src/main/runtime and nowhere in mobile/. So the boundary it disappears at is the HOST PROJECTION: the runtime never picks it up onto the row it publishes, and mobile can therefore produce 'inferred' but never 'recorded'. P2, not higher, because no mobile surface consumes the distinction: there is no relaunch-from-this-tab affordance anywhere in mobile/src or mobile/app, so nothing currently presents an inference as a fact. That is what makes this a gap to close BEFORE mobile grows such an affordance, rather than before release. What closes it: carry launchProfileId on the published row and derive on the client, since the derivation module is pure TypeScript with type-only imports and is portable as-is.",
      "notes": "Two instrument failures worth keeping, because the first nearly produced a confident wrong answer. A repo-wide ripgrep over src/ TIMED OUT and returned a PARTIAL result WITHOUT saying so -- it omitted a file I had already read and knew contained the token, which is the only reason the truncation was caught. The same search then failed loudly on a retry. Every claim above was re-taken with a different instrument carrying a positive control against a file known to contain the term. An absence is only worth what the instrument that found it is worth."
    },
    {
      "id": "session.lifecycle-vocabulary",
      "title": "The user can tell a restored session from one whose history could not be resumed",
      "disposition": "ported",
      "evidence": [
        "src/main/runtime/orca-runtime.test.ts",
        "mobile/src/worktree/agent-row-resume-provenance.test.ts",
        "mobile/src/components/WorktreeListRow.test.ts"
      ],
      "notes": "The old title named a four-way vocabulary Orca does not have. Desktop's actual user-facing vocabulary is two values, 'restored' and 'resume-unavailable' (session-restored-banner-pane-state.ts), driven by an agentResumeUnavailable flag the PTY controller sets when a pane asked to resume a provider session, the resume was dropped as unverifiable, and a fresh session launched instead. A previous note here claimed that flag has ZERO references under src/main/runtime; re-measured 2026-09-08 that is wrong -- orca-runtime.ts declares it on the controller interface it holds and never reads it, so the fact already arrives at the runtime that serves mobile and is discarded there. That makes this the same shape as terminal.launch-profile-offer and session.unconfirmed-activity: available and unconsumed, rather than absent. Mobile therefore cannot tell a session whose history survived from one wearing its clothes. sessionBoundary is separately not a mobile concern: mobile has no `state === 'done'` branch in any non-test source, so there is no completion consumer to mislead. CLOSED 2026-09-08: the flag is retained on the per-PTY record (so it cannot outlive its subject) and projected onto the agent row beside restoredUnconfirmed, which answers a different question -- that one is whether the host vouches for the activity, this one whether the history survived, and the tests pin that they move independently. Mobile reads it three-valued, because an older host omitting the field is byte-identical to a newer host reporting universal success: absence means 'resumed' only when agent-session.resume-provenance.v1 is advertised, and otherwise means unknown, where mobile refuses the continuity claim. That ambiguity is why an additive optional field still needed a negotiated capability."
    },
    {
      "id": "session.unconfirmed-activity-is-not-presented-as-live",
      "title": "A row the host could not confirm is not shown as a working agent",
      "disposition": "ported",
      "evidence": ["mobile/src/worktree/agent-row-unconfirmed-provenance.test.ts"],
      "notes": "restoredUnconfirmed rides on RuntimeWorktreeAgentRow and says the host hydrated a nonterminal row from disk with no live hook since. Mobile dropped it -- it was not in agentDotState's Pick -- so a turn that may have ended hours ago rendered as Working until a 30-minute clock expired on a timestamp that was never evidence of liveness. Mobile now uses the desktop's own isFreshNonDoneAgentStatus instead of a local mirror; the mirror is how the clause went missing, having copied the timeout and not the condition. The list equality was extended to compare the field, because a field the renderer reads must be one the change check sees."
    },
    {
      "id": "session.background-does-not-stop-remote-work",
      "title": "Backgrounding the control client does not stop host-owned work",
      "disposition": "superseded",
      "reason": "Mobile already holds this: the host PTY outlives the app, and mobile re-subscribes on foreground (mobile/src/terminal/terminal-foreground-recovery.ts). The webview backing store dies, not the remote work. Nothing to port; what is missing is a proof, tracked under session.lifecycle-vocabulary."
    },
    {
      "id": "transport.reconnect-and-command-deduplication",
      "title": "A dropped connection recovers without duplicating commands",
      "disposition": "superseded",
      "reason": "Mobile's transport is ahead of the desktop's here and was never a fork target: E2EE v2, relay/direct upgrade with hysteresis, a reconnect controller, a resume director, a session-liveness watchdog, and request-single-flight. Porting desktop semantics onto this would be a regression."
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace.repo-and-worktree-are-distinct",
      "title": "Selecting a worktree acts on that worktree, not on the repo's primary checkout",
      "disposition": "shared-already",
      "evidence": ["mobile/src/worktree/workspace-list-types.ts"],
      "notes": "repoId and worktreeId are distinct fields on mobile with full lineage modelling, so the flattening risk this family exists to catch does not apply."
    },
    {
      "id": "source-control.actions",
      "title": "Source control is usable, not merely viewable",
      "disposition": "superseded",
      "reason": "Mobile is at action parity, not view parity: stage, commit, push with --force-with-lease, pull, PR, branch-compare and conflict-abort all exist. No porting owed."
    },
    {
      "id": "voice.no-auto-submit",
      "title": "A transcript is never submitted to an agent without the user acting",
      "disposition": "shared-already",
      "evidence": ["mobile/app.json"],
      "notes": "Mobile streams microphone audio to the paired desktop over speech.* RPC and inserts the transcript without submitting it; no auto-submit path exists on either variant. ASR placement differs (host-local for mobile) but the safety contract is identical."
    },
    {
      "id": "voice.transcript-target-ownership",
      "title": "A transcript belongs to the input that started recording, not the one focused when it lands",
      "disposition": "ported",
      "evidence": [
        "src/renderer/src/components/dictation/DictationController.tsx",
        "mobile/src/dictation/mobile-dictation-delivery.ts",
        "mobile/src/dictation/mobile-dictation-transcript-dispatch.ts",
        "mobile/src/dictation/mobile-dictation-transcript-dispatch.test.ts",
        "mobile/src/session/mobile-native-chat-captured-draft-writes.ts",
        "mobile/src/session/use-mobile-native-chat-drafts-dictation-target.test.ts"
      ],
      "reason": "Closed 2026-09-16, in two steps, because the first fix captured a surface CATEGORY where an INSTANCE was required. b8c229ce0 stopped delivery reading showNativeChatRef and made it consume a target captured at startDictation -- but that target recorded { surface: 'native-chat' } and nothing more, so the chat destination was still re-derived at delivery from the current tab. Both tabs follow a chat default, so switching tabs during ASR still moved the words into another agent's composer: the same defect one layer down, and reachable under default configuration. 163748a30 captures the draft key itself (host + worktree + tab, the identity MobileNativeChatSendOrigin already used for sends), and captureDictationTarget now REFUSES a visible chat it cannot name rather than capturing it as a category. resolveDictationDelivery takes no current-surface argument at all, so there is nothing mutable in scope to retarget from. All three original sequences are closed: terminal-then-chat, chat tab A then tab B, and chat-then-terminal.",
      "notes": "Evidence classes, kept apart on purpose. BEHAVIORAL INTEGRATION: mobile-dictation-transcript-dispatch.test.ts (7 cases) composes the real dispatcher with the real drafts hook, and use-mobile-native-chat-drafts-dictation-target.test.ts (5) drives the hook directly. MUTATION: pointing the chat write at a key other than the captured one reds 2 behavioural cases with 5 liveness controls green; restore verified byte-exact by SHA-256. SOURCE-SHAPE, and only this: that the session route hands the hook's captured-key writer to the dispatcher. That last one used to cover the whole dispatch -- severing the composition reddened one string assertion while every behavioural case stayed green -- which is why the dispatch was lifted out of the 5327-line route into a module that can be driven. NOT PROVEN: no Product Reality run on a device or emulator; the test phone has no route to the host. Retention of an unclaimed transcript was described here as 'the second half' and is NOT closed by this entry -- it is a different capability and now has its own row, voice.unclaimed-transcript-recovery, so the unresolved total is unchanged by this closure."
    },
    {
      "id": "voice.unclaimed-transcript-recovery",
      "title": "Words the user successfully spoke are recoverable when their destination no longer exists",
      "disposition": "measured-open",
      "severity": "P2",
      "severityReason": "Nothing is misdelivered and nothing is falsely reported: the refusal is truthful, the words reach no other agent, and no success is claimed. What is lost is one dictation the user can repeat, and only in the window where the captured subject dies mid-ASR. It is P2 rather than P1 because the harmful outcome this used to share a row with -- text landing on another agent's composer -- is closed, and P2 rather than P3 because the lost material is the user's own composed intent rather than something re-derivable.",
      "evidence": [
        "src/renderer/src/components/dictation/DictationController.tsx",
        "mobile/src/dictation/mobile-dictation-transcript-dispatch.ts",
        "mobile/src/dictation/mobile-dictation-transcript-dispatch.test.ts"
      ],
      "reason": "Split out of voice.transcript-target-ownership on 2026-09-16, where it had been carried inside the reason prose as 'the second half' and was therefore uncountable. Desktop has it: a transcript the origin cannot claim is RETAINED and the user is told. Mobile refuses and stops. Two shapes, both pinned behaviourally as SAFE rather than as recovered: a capture that never happened reports 'Dictation had no target' and writes to no surface, and a captured PTY that will not take the bytes produces no second success notice and no write anywhere else. That refusal is correct and must not be weakened to close this row -- the missing capability is recoverability, not delivery. What closes it: a surface that holds successfully recognised text whose destination has gone, so the user can put it somewhere. Deliberately NOT built alongside the target-ownership fix: choosing between a retained draft, a notice carrying the text, and a re-target picker is a product-semantics decision, and no existing mobile unsent-intent recovery surface was found to connect to.",
      "notes": "Do not fold this back into target ownership. They fail differently and are fixed differently: one is about WHERE words go, and is closed; this one is about whether words the user already spoke can be got back, and is not. Sequenced after the Terminal Inspector wave rather than before it, because the recovery surface is UI that should be designed once."
    },
    {
      "id": "navigation.reversible-navigation-preserves-context",
      "title": "Leaving a work surface and coming back preserves the scene rather than recreating it",
      "disposition": "shared-already",
      "evidence": ["mobile/src/navigation/screen-context-preservation.test.ts"],
      "notes": "Re-measured 2026-09-10 and unchanged: no screen or layout under mobile/app sets unmountOnBlur, freezeOnBlur, unmountOnExit or detachInactiveScreens, so mobile holds this the same way desktop does. What was missing was never the capability, only the proof -- and the proof had to read mobile/app from disk, because vitest.config.ts includes src/**/*.test.ts and collects nothing from that tree at all. A property held by ABSENCE cannot fail a suite while it is true, so the sweep asserts a floor of screens found and drives its own matcher against a synthetic source; a sweep that stopped matching would otherwise report exactly what a clean one reports."
    },
    {
      "id": "drafts.user-invested-input-survives-process-death",
      "title": "Text the user composed and has not sent survives the app being killed",
      "disposition": "measured-open",
      "severity": "P1",
      "severityReason": "It takes the OS killing the app to reach, and what is lost is the user's own unsent input rather than a file on disk -- nothing already saved or committed is worse off, which is what separates this from the discard P0 beside it.",
      "evidence": ["mobile/src/files/MobileFilePreviewScreen.tsx"],
      "reason": "Confirmed 2026-09-10 with an instrument wide enough to answer it. The earlier note said 'no persistence site was found', which is only worth something if the search could have found one -- mobile/src/storage exists, so the check was re-run across every source in mobile/src/files for an import of that module as well as for the storage APIs themselves. There are none. The draft lives in useState with two refs mirroring it, and the screen's only protection is an in-session guard that warns before navigating away, which is a different hazard from the one this capability names. Process death discards composed, unsent text with no notice. P1 rather than P0: it takes the OS killing the app, and what is lost is the user's own unsent input rather than a file on disk. What closes it: a durability tier in config/durability-contracts.jsonc naming the state and its consequence, then a write that satisfies it -- not a mobile-only draft taxonomy, since 'what does this write owe if it is lost' already has an owner in this repository."
    },
    {
      "id": "runtime.mutation-carries-its-own-context",
      "title": "A mobile mutation names the workspace it acts on, and is refused without one",
      "disposition": "shared-already",
      "evidence": ["src/main/runtime/mobile-rpc-allowlist.test.ts"],
      "notes": "One wire schema serves both variants. Measured 2026-09-08: the RPC method directory holds no BrowserWindow reference, and of the 40+ state-changing methods mobile can reach, all but four reject a request naming no workspace -- settings.update and ui.set are host-global by design, terminal.multiplex routes by streamId, and terminal.create leaves worktree optional and is not on any mobile path. The test enumerates by mutation verb rather than by looking for the context check, so it can find a method that lacks one."
    },
    {
      "id": "runtime.command-acts-on-the-window-that-owns-the-target",
      "title": "A remote command reaches the workspace it named, not a representative one",
      "disposition": "measured-open",
      "severity": "P1",
      "severityReason": "It needs two or more windows AND a target no window has placed. Past that point the chosen window still has to find the tab at all, which bounds the blast radius -- and that bound is itself unmeasured, so this severity is the weakest of the four and the first to revisit.",
      "evidence": [
        "src/main/runtime/runtime-notifier-router.ts",
        "src/main/runtime/runtime-graph-owners.ts"
      ],
      "reason": "Measured 2026-09-10, and the answer is a disagreement between two adjacent layers rather than a missing check. runtime-graph-owners resolves an unplaced worktree or tab to the SOLE window when there is exactly one, and to null when there are several -- its comment says refusing to guess is the safe direction, and it is right. orca-runtime notifiersFor then turns that null into every window, defended by its own comment on the grounds that dropping a command silently would be worse than delivering it widely. Both are reasonable; composed, the second inverts the first. For a notification the wide delivery is likely still correct. What makes this open is that four entries past that point do not notify, they ACT, and each takes the first window off that fallback list: revealTerminalSession, readMobileMarkdownTab, saveMobileMarkdownTab and closeTerminalTab. So with two or more windows and a target no window has placed, a mobile-initiated markdown save or terminal-tab close is performed by whichever window happens to be enumerated first. P1, not P0: it needs a second window plus an unplaced target, and the blast radius past that point is bounded by whether the chosen window can find the tab at all -- which is exactly what nobody has measured. What closes it: separate the no-op branch from the broadcast branch, so a verb that acts refuses an unplaceable target instead of inheriting a fallback designed for verbs that inform.",
      "notes": "The general shape is the one in scoped-side-effect-authority: composing two safety inputs requires writing down each gate's no-op branch, because a gate that only fires in one state switches OFF rather than tightening when the state changes. Here the graph-owner layer's safe answer is null, and null is precisely the value the next layer reads as 'everyone'."
    },
    {
      "id": "authority.repository-mutations-are-classified-by-stale-consequence",
      "title": "Every repository mutation a client can reach says what a stale caller does to it",
      "disposition": "shared-already",
      "evidence": ["src/main/runtime/mobile-git-mutation-consequence.test.ts"],
      "notes": "One wire schema serves both variants, so the classification is the product's, not mobile's. Each git and worktree method mobile can reach is a read or a mutation with a named protection -- compare-and-swap, guarded-by-git, converges-on-repeat, operation-id, or recoverable -- and a method in neither list fails the sweep, so a newly allowlisted mutation cannot inherit the subsystem's reputation without being looked at. The sweep's red branch is driven by a test, and the unprotected set is shrink-only."
    },
    {
      "id": "authority.stale-client-cannot-overwrite-current-state",
      "title": "A client that reconnects after missing changes adopts current state instead of publishing its own",
      "disposition": "ported",
      "evidence": [
        "src/main/runtime/mobile-git-mutation-consequence.test.ts",
        "src/main/runtime/git-discard-stale-authorization.test.ts",
        "src/main/git/status-worktree-content-identity.test.ts",
        "src/main/runtime/mobile-discard-observed-state-required.test.ts",
        "mobile/src/source-control/discard-authorization.test.ts",
        "mobile/src/session/mobile-diff-review-queue-discard-identity.test.ts"
      ],
      "reason": "CLOSED 2026-09-10 as a vertical slice, having been deliberately left half-open on 2026-09-08 rather than half-shipped. git.discard was the one mobile-reachable mutation whose stale case destroyed state absent from the object graph. Now: status stamps each changed entry with the identity of its working-tree bytes when the caller asks (opt-in, measured at 1-4% of a refresh's existing git cost at 5-25 changed files); both mobile discard surfaces cite the row they showed, through one shared resolver; the host re-reads the bytes immediately before destroying them and refuses if they moved; and a paired client that states nothing is refused outright rather than falling back to the old behaviour. The identity is NOT the blob oids status prints for free -- those name HEAD and the index, and a discard leaves the index alone, so they identify states it does not destroy; a test pins that the chosen identity changes across an edit where they and the line counts do not.",
      "notes": "The last sentence of this note used to read 'desktop-vs-mobile git and worktree mutations are unfenced'. Measured 2026-09-08, that is wrong. Four mechanisms cover the surface: a required compare-and-swap on a ref (worktree.forceDeleteBranch/expectedHead, git.forkSync/expectedUpstream), git's own refusal of a non-fast-forward or a work-losing checkout, --force-with-lease on the force push, and clientMutationId on the creates. Every mobile-reachable git and worktree method is now classified in src/main/runtime/mobile-git-mutation-consequence.test.ts, and one comes out unprotected: git.discard destroys uncommitted changes at a path with no statement of what the caller believed was there, and mobile calls it live from two surfaces. Every other stale case is recoverable from the object graph; uncommitted work is not. The 2026-09-08 note ended by saying closing it needed 'a per-file identity the porcelain parser currently discards'. That was also wrong, and it is the more useful correction: the parser discards HEAD and index oids, and neither describes the working-tree bytes a discard destroys -- git never hashes the working file during status at all. Adopting the identity that was free would have produced a check that passes a file edited twice against an unchanged index. The lesson is general enough to carry: an identity a system offers cheaply is an identity of something it already had a reason to compute, which is rarely the thing your operation acts on."
    },
    {
      "id": "product.display-name",
      "title": "Both variants present the product under its current name",
      "disposition": "ported",
      "evidence": [
        "mobile/src/diagnostics/connection-diagnostics-report.test.ts",
        "mobile/src/components/HostProtocolGate.test.ts"
      ],
      "notes": "Desktop is 'Orca X' via src/shared/product.ts. Mobile named itself 'Orca' on its icon, about screen, onboarding, diagnostics and OS permission prompts, and called the desktop 'Orca' throughout its prose. Both halves are done; doing only the first would have reproduced the split-label defect this registry opened on. The rename deliberately does not reach scheme 'orca', bundle id com.stably.orca.mobile, slug orca-mobile, or the 'orca:' storage-key prefix -- those are what deep links, the stores and already-paired devices resolve against."
    }
  ]
}
